When creating an RMI or JMX connector server without properly configuring
the environment map with credential type restrictions, attackers can exploit
the Java deserialization mechanism in the authentication process.

Example (vulnerable):
```java
// Null environment - vulnerable
JMXConnectorServer cs = JMXConnectorServerFactory.newJMXConnectorServer(url, null, mbs);

// Empty environment - also vulnerable
Map<String, Object> env = new HashMap<>();
RMIConnectorServer rcs = new RMIConnectorServer(url, env, mbs);
```

Safer approach:
```java
Map<String, Object> env = new HashMap<>();
env.put("jmx.remote.rmi.server.credential.types",
    new String[]{String[].class.getName()});
RMIConnectorServer rcs = new RMIConnectorServer(url, env, mbs);
```

To remediate this issue:
- Set jmx.remote.rmi.server.credential.types in the environment map
- Or use jmx.remote.rmi.server.credentials.filter.pattern for filtering
- Restrict accepted credential types to java.lang.String

