Basic authentication only obfuscates the username and password using Base64 encoding,
which can be easily decoded. When transmitted over plaintext HTTP, credentials are
vulnerable to interception via man-in-the-middle attacks or packet sniffing.

This rule detects code that:
1. Opens an HTTP (non-HTTPS) connection
2. Sets an Authorization header with Basic authentication

### Vulnerable code example

```java
// BAD: Basic auth over HTTP
String url = "http://api.example.com/data";
HttpURLConnection conn = (HttpURLConnection) new URL(url).openConnection();
String auth = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString("user:pass".getBytes());
conn.setRequestProperty("Authorization", "Basic " + auth);
```

### Remediation

Always use HTTPS when transmitting credentials:

```java
// GOOD: Basic auth over HTTPS
String url = "https://api.example.com/data";
HttpURLConnection conn = (HttpURLConnection) new URL(url).openConnection();
String auth = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString("user:pass".getBytes());
conn.setRequestProperty("Authorization", "Basic " + auth);
```

Additional recommendations:
- Use modern authentication methods like OAuth 2.0 or API tokens when possible
- Ensure TLS/SSL certificates are properly validated
- Consider certificate pinning for sensitive applications

