XSLT injection occurs when an attacker can control the XSLT stylesheet used in a transformation.
XSLT is a powerful language that can:
- Execute arbitrary Java code via extension functions
- Read local files using document() function
- Make network requests
- Cause denial of service

Example (vulnerable):
```java
@PostMapping("/transform")
public String transform(@RequestParam String stylesheet, @RequestBody String xml) {
    StreamSource xsltSource = new StreamSource(new StringReader(stylesheet));
    TransformerFactory factory = TransformerFactory.newInstance();
    Transformer transformer = factory.newTransformer(xsltSource);
    // Attacker controls stylesheet
}
```

To remediate this issue:
- Never allow users to supply XSLT stylesheets
- Use only trusted, pre-defined stylesheets
- If user customization is needed, use a safe templating approach
- Disable XSLT extension functions if possible

